@prefix sensing: <http://example.org/orexis/sensing#> .
@prefix water: <http://example.org/orexis/water#> .
@prefix mqtt: <http://example.org/orexis/mqtt#> .
@prefix ag:   <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix sh:   <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .

<http://example.org/orexis/core> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Orexis core" ;
    rdfs:comment """The society kernel: agents, the capabilities they compose, and the versioned world they share. Everything else is a MODULE layered on this — the microcontrollers it runs on, the protocols their parts speak, the parts themselves, the capabilities, and the domain. Code may reference the terms defined here; it may never reference an instance.

What is NOT here any more is everything physical. `packages/part/microcontroller` holds boards, peripherals, pins and wires; a concrete part is its own package beside it. The two subjects were sharing a file and growing at different rates: adding a sensor should not touch what a society IS.""" .

#################  Agents and capabilities (composition, not hierarchy)  #################

ag:Agent a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Agent" ;
    rdfs:comment "A self-interested principal with a stake. What an agent can DO is not its class — it is the set of capabilities composed onto it, so two agents of the same kind may run different modules." .

ag:Capability a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Capability" ;
    rdfs:comment "One thing an agent can do, defined by a module: a vocabulary (this ontology), rules (its SHACL shapes), and the code that reads them. Declaring a capability is what activates the module." .

ag:hasCapability a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ; rdfs:range ag:Capability ;
    rdfs:label "has capability" ;
    rdfs:comment "Composed onto the agent in the WORLD graph (public: peers must know what a counterparty can do). The PARAMETERS of a capability are private, in the agent's own beliefs." .

ag:actsFor a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "acts for" ;
    rdfs:comment "The subject whose interest this agent advances. Generic; a domain narrows the range (in the water domain, a Plant)." .

#################  Devices — dumb mechanism at the edge  #################

ag:Device a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Device" ;
    rdfs:comment "A physical edge node: mechanism, never policy, and interchangeable with a simulated one." .

#################  Devices that are not there  #################
# A simulated device is an ORDINARY device in every statement the world makes about it: it is
# on a bus, it has topics, it has a sense mode, and an agent is wired to it with sensing:polls like
# any other. The only extra fact is that a process stands in for it.
#
# The marker is on the DEVICE and never on the agent, and that is the whole design. An agent
# that could tell would be a second code path, and a second code path is what a simulation is
# supposed to avoid: world/simulation used to exercise its own implementation, so it could pass
# while the real one was broken.

ag:simulatedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Device ; rdfs:range ag:DeviceModel ;
    rdfs:label "simulated by" ;
    rdfs:comment "No such device exists; a process stands in for it, publishing on the same topics and speaking the same protocol. Read by the generators, which give it a container and a credential, and by nothing an agent runs — an agent must not be able to tell, and the surest way is that nothing it executes ever asks." .

ag:modelMinValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ; rdfs:label "model minimum" ;
    rdfs:comment "The floor of what this stand-in can report. A moisture fraction happens to run 0..1; a thermometer does not, so the range is stated rather than assumed." .
ag:modelMaxValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ; rdfs:label "model maximum" .

ag:DeviceModel a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Device model" ;
    rdfs:comment "The physics the stand-in computes between readings. Facts about the thing being modelled rather than opinions about it, which is why they sit on the device and not in anybody's beliefs." .

ag:modelInitialValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:DeviceModel ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "model initial value" ;
    rdfs:comment "What it reads before anything has happened to it." .

ag:modelDoseEffect a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "model dose effect" ;
    rdfs:comment "How a dispensed unit moves this modelled value, SIGNED — in units of this value per litre on the dose wire, the litre being the WIRE's unit (a valve's status message speaks ml; that is actuation's physical reality, so it lives in this comment and not in the term's name — the first draft said modelDoseLitres, and the kernel wearing the water domain's word is the exact smell modelLosesPerDay had just been cured of). A pot's conversion arrives through the domain's denomination and never through this; a SOURCE'S effect is not per-instance configuration at all but CONSERVATION — every source loses exactly what was dispensed from it — so the water domain states -1.0 once, on the WaterSource class, and the closure entails it onto every barrel. Read from the SUBJECT by the generation, like the ceiling and the drying." .

ag:modelLosesPerDay a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    # Un-domained since #164: stated on a DeviceModel as an override, or entailed onto a
    # SUBJECT from a domain's own physics term (water:driesPerDay rdfs:subPropertyOf this),
    # so the generator reads one kernel term and the pot keeps the only copy of its fact.
    rdfs:label "model dries per day" ;
    rdfs:comment "How much of the observed property is LOST per simulated day — first-order decay toward the model's floor, for any value that drains: a pot's moisture, a barrel's evaporation, a battery's self-discharge. Was modelDriesPerDay, a kernel term wearing the water domain's word (every-term-in-its-own-house had it listed: only a plant dries); before that ag:modelDryRate, per tick, and the tick made the physics an artifact of attention: the pot dried one step per reading, so a closely-watched plant dried faster than an ignored one — backwards, and visibly so once the cadence machinery started varying how closely anyone watched. Time-based now, scaled by the world's ag:timeScale, so the pot loses what the day costs it however often it is read. Since #164 the domain's water:driesPerDay is a subproperty of this: a pot states its physics once, in its own vocabulary, and the closure entails the kernel's term the generation reads — while a DeviceModel may still state this directly, as an override for values that are not anyone's pot." .

ag:modelDailySwing a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:DeviceModel ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "model daily swing" ;
    rdfs:comment "The amplitude of a daily sine around the value — for properties with a diurnal cycle, which soil moisture does not have and air temperature does. One simulated day per period, so under ag:timeScale 24 a bench hour draws a whole day's curve. Absent means flat, which is what the old ever-falling drift pretended not to be." .

ag:modelTickSeconds a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:DeviceModel ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "model tick seconds" ;
    rdfs:comment "Wall-clock seconds per tick — the PUBLISH interval of a sensing:PushProcedure device, which keeps its own clock; a sensing:ScheduledProcedure one keeps the interval its agent gives it, exactly as a board does. This used to also pace the physics, which is the artifact ag:modelLosesPerDay records; physics run on the clock now, and the tick is only a rhythm of speech." .

ag:timeScale a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:World ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "time scale" ;
    rdfs:comment "Simulated seconds per wall-clock second — 24 makes one bench hour a simulated day. A fact about the WORLD, not any device: every stand-in in a world must age at the same rate or their physics stop composing (a dose sized for a day's drying must land against a day's drying). The agents are deliberately not told: they act in real time against a world that happens to live fast, which changes nothing about how they decide and everything about how much of their behaviour a bench hour shows." .

ag:strayDoseMeanDays a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:World ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "stray dose mean days" ;
    rdfs:comment "The mean interval, in simulated days, between waterings NOBODY in the society decided — a human with a watering can, rain through a window. Exponentially distributed, per subject that states an ag:rainTopic. Absent means nobody meddles. Stating it gives the world ONE meddler: a container of its own (firmware/simulated-meddler) with a credential of its own, granted WRITE on each rain topic and nothing else — a pot must not water itself, and rain must never ride the valve's status topic, which is the valve's testimony. This is the third-party impulse of #151 made routine: the signal changes without any agent's doing, and the society's only defence is how it samples." .

ag:rainTopic a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "rain topic" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where water from OUTSIDE the society arrives for this subject — the meddler writes it, the subject's simulated sensor reads it, and nothing else may touch it. On the SUBJECT, because rain falls on a pot and not on an instrument; a simulated world without one for a subject keeps that subject dry except by the market. The payload is the dose report's shape ({\"ml\": N}) because millilitres are millilitres to soil — the indistinguishability is the point, not an economy." .

#################  Where a society executes  #################
ag:ComputeHost a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Device ; rdfs:label "Compute host" ;
    rdfs:comment "A machine that runs agent processes. Named so the world can say where its society actually executes." .

ag:lanHost a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:ComputeHost ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "LAN host" ;
    rdfs:comment """How this host is reached by things NOT on it. A co-located agent reaches the broker at mqtt:brokerHost, which is loopback and must be, because host-networked agents have to agree on one name. A board on the wifi cannot use that name, so it needs a way to the machine running the broker. Two routes to one broker, and only the second is a fact about the network rather than about the society.

**A NAME, and that is the whole of why this is not `lanHost` any more.** An address here was a DHCP lease, and the old comment admitted it: "a fact with an expiry date". It expired three times — .126, then .130, then .133 — and the third cost a day, because a board went on dialling .130 and simply fell silent. Nothing in a world can be true about an address a router is free to reassign.

A hostname does not expire. `raspberry.local` is answered by the avahi already running on that host, and ESP32's lwIP resolves `.local` because `CONFIG_LWIP_DNS_SUPPORT_MDNS_QUERIES` is set in the framework this project builds against — so nothing in the firmware changes: `mqtt.setServer` has always taken a name. A router's own domain (`raspberry.speedport.ip` here) works equally well over ordinary DNS and is the fallback if multicast is ever blocked; it is not the default because it couples a world to a brand of router.

An address is still a legal value — some networks have no name service — and stating one is choosing to own the consequence.""" .

ag:runsOn a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range ag:ComputeHost ; rdfs:label "runs on" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which host executes this agent. Documents why the bus is where it is." .

#################  Naming — stated, never derived  #################

ag:localId a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "local id" ;
    rdfs:comment "The short name a resource is known by outside RDF. Stated here so nothing has to infer it from the shape of a URI. The ONE instance identifier a process is given is an agent's localId; everything else it discovers." .

#################  The store every agent keeps, and what it may cost  #################
#  Not a capability, and the distinction is the one AGENTS.md rule 2 draws: a capability is an
#  ability whose HOW could differ. Reviewing can be done by rule or by asking a model; compacting
#  cannot be done two ways. Keeping your own house is not a choice, so it is kernel — and it has
#  a clock no capability owns, because an agent granted no room to review itself must still
#  compact. See orexis/upkeep.py and issue #45.

ag:BeliefBase a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Belief base" ;
    rdfs:comment "The store one agent keeps everything it knows in — the world it was given, what it has sensed, and its own beliefs. A class rather than a graph, because it is the whole store and not one graph inside it: what the agent BELIEVES is the sum of them. Named so that facts true of every agent's store have somewhere to be stated that no capability owns." .

ag:maxBytesPerTriple a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "max bytes per triple" ;
    rdfs:comment "Above this ratio a belief base is mostly write amplification rather than knowledge, and is compacted. Stated here rather than compiled in, for the same reason the cadence bounds are: it is a fact about what this society tolerates, not about how the code happens to be written. It hung off the review capability until that left the kernel — a threshold every agent is held to cannot be owned by a package an agent may not have." .

ag:BeliefBase ag:maxBytesPerTriple 800 .

ag:resignAfterS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "resign after seconds" ;
    rdfs:comment "How long an agent endures a dead bus before it resigns and lets its container bring it back fresh (#53). The fault this bounds is the one that cost two days: a session that ends and never returns, inside a container that looks perfectly healthy, on a bus whose broker never restarted. Ten minutes is far past any reconnect paho would ever achieve — a link that flaps recovers in seconds and never meets this — and far under the hours a quiet failure used to cost. Stated here rather than compiled in, like the compaction ratio above: it is what this society tolerates, not how the code happens to be written." .

ag:Agent ag:resignAfterS 600 .

#################  Identity keys — published, because a verifier cannot read secrets  #########
#
#  The public halves of each agent's keypairs (#144, #145), written by `orexis-keygen` into a
#  generated `keys.ttl` beside the world's ratified files. Public by nature and in the world
#  because the world is the one place every member already reads: the host verifying a
#  presentation cannot read the presenter's secrets — that is the isolation — so the roster is
#  how identities are attested. Base64 of the raw 32 bytes, no PEM ceremony.
#
#  TWO keys because the operations differ and conflating them is the classic mistake: Ed25519
#  SIGNS (a presentation under the winner's own hand, #144) and X25519 SEALS (a claim only
#  its winner can open, #145). Absence is the pre-key era and stays legal — a world onboarded
#  before keygen learned agents behaves exactly as it always did.

ag:signingKey a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "signing key" ;
    rdfs:comment "Ed25519 public key, base64 raw. What a presentation is verified against: the durable identity exercising an ephemeral grant, per knowledge/decisions/authn-authz-capabilities.md." .
ag:sealingKey a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "sealing key" ;
    rdfs:comment "X25519 public key, base64 raw. What a claim is sealed to, so the bus carries an envelope only the winner can open — the ACL keeps agents out, the seal keeps the bus out." .

#################  Who did it, and in what capacity  #################
#  A ROLE, not an identity, and the distinction is the whole point. There is no `ag:Sovereign`
#  agent and there must not be: an installation has several users — one authors a world, another
#  operates it with fewer powers — and which of them was sovereign is a fact about ONE ACTIVITY,
#  not a permanent property of a person. PROV-O models exactly this with `prov:qualifiedAssociation`,
#  and expects the domain to name its own roles; naming one here is using PROV-O, not replacing it.
#
#  The role set is open on purpose. `ag:Operator` and whatever else will be needed are absent
#  because nothing distinguishes them yet, and a role no activity cites is speculation.

ag:Sovereign a prov:Role ; rdfs:label "Sovereign" ;
    rdfs:comment "Held by whoever ratified a world: authored its files and stands behind them. A role a user has in one ratification, never a kind of user — the same person may hold it for one world and not another, and an installation's other users hold none. See knowledge/decisions/who-put-the-fact-there.md." .

#################  The world and its versions  #################

ag:World a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "World" ;
    rdfs:comment "The ratified world produced by genesis. Found by TYPE, never by a well-known IRI." .
ag:WorldVersion a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "World version" ;
    rdfs:comment "One ratified version of the world's topology; the chain is append-only." .
ag:currentVersion a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:World ; rdfs:range ag:WorldVersion ;
    rdfs:label "current version" .
ag:versionNumber a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:WorldVersion ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "version number" .
ag:underWorldVersion a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "under world version" ;
    rdfs:comment "The world-version in force when this fact was recorded (provenance)." .

#################  Graphs — typed, self-describing (not magic strings)  #################
#
#  A graph IRI is an INSTANCE. `ag:WorldGraph` is the term code may name;
#  `…/graph/world` is a particular graph and code may not. The classes below are the terms;
#  the instances at the end of this section are the data — and the split is what lets a reader
#  ask for "public knowledge" without any Python knowing which graphs those happen to be.
#
#  They are declared HERE rather than in each world because they are the same in every world:
#  installation-wide, like the shape of the store itself. Only the per-agent ones vary, and
#  those are derived from the roster (rules.ru) rather than written out.

ag:Graph a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Named graph" .

#################  Three axes over one graph  #################
#
#  A named graph is classified THREE ways, independently, and conflating them is why some
#  graphs never found a natural home: what its content ASSERTS, WHOSE it is, and HOW IT
#  ARRIVED. A species' range is a constraint AND public AND derived; an aim is a desire AND
#  private AND asserted; an obligation is a constraint AND private AND received. Nothing but
#  the first axis is about meaning, and the first axis is the one the vocabulary lacked.
#
#  See knowledge/decisions/the-mind-is-six-graphs.md. The classes below say what a graph IS;
#  which graphs are instances of each is data, asked for and never listed — the arrangement
#  ag:PublicGraph already had, generalised to six modalities and five arrivals.

#  --- modality: what the content asserts ---

ag:Modality a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Modality" ;
    rdfs:comment "What a graph's content asserts about the subjects in it. The same subject, property and unit appear in every modality — fern's moisture is observed, bounded, aimed at, moved by a lever, committed to and recorded — so the modality is what a reader must know to interpret a triple, and the one thing the graph vocabulary could not previously say." .

ag:BeliefGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Graph ; rdfs:label "Belief graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "What IS: the world as ratified, what the rules and the closure make of it, and what instruments report. NOT the graph historically called `beliefs`, which holds picks and no beliefs at all — that misnaming is what naming the axis exposed." .




ag:PickRecordGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Graph ; rdfs:label "Pick record graph" ;
    ag:graphPrefix "http://example.org/orexis/graph/beliefs/" ;
    rdfs:comment "ONE agent's record of picking: what birth authored and review has since re-picked, one value per term, plus the belief-proper terms (conversions, the wallet) the #297 sort left beside them. A RECORD class, not a modality — the desire modality derives its wants and projects this record in as a premise-and-content, so what used to need a modality typing to be copied is now read by name, the one construction from an agent's own id the rules allow. The write boundary is ag:beliefsOf, unchanged." .

ag:ObligationsGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Graph ; rdfs:label "Obligations record graph" ;
    ag:graphPrefix "http://example.org/orexis/graph/obligations/" ;
    rdfs:comment "ONE agent's record of debts: claims won against it, presented or not, discharged rows kept because a debt paid and a debt forgotten must not look alike. A RECORD class — the duty-wants the desire modality serves are this record projected, and the prefix moved here when ag:ConstraintGraph retired (#312)." .

#################  What kind of menu row this is  #################
#
#  A menu row says what happens THROUGH an agent, and there are two kinds. A CHOSEN row is an
#  option: mine, at my discretion. An HONOURED row is a lever others may demand — disclosed so
#  an agent's conduct surface is legible whole, which the desire-scoped menu could not do: an
#  ability that serves others was a true fact living nowhere queryable.
#
#  DISCLOSURE, not exemption. What an obligation makes an agent do arrives as a WANT — a desire
#  it did not source, with provenance saying whose it is — so duties and wants share one
#  currency and one decision path. See
#  knowledge/decisions/an-obligation-is-a-desire-someone-else-sourced.md.
#
#  THE KERNEL'S, and these words have now made the same move twice for the same reason. They
#  were written in the market package and left it because "a mode is a fact about a menu row,
#  and the menu is the deliberation package's". The menu is the kernel's, so they follow it
#  again — and the argument is stronger the second time: sensing, actuation and market EACH
#  bind `Chosen` or `Honoured` in their own `affordances.rq` to describe their own rows. Three
#  packages needing a word to say what they themselves offer is the test the-mind-is-six-graphs
#  set, and it is the test `ag:Intention` passed.

ag:Mode a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Mode" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether a row is mine to choose or a lever others may demand. Two individuals and no third: a row nobody may ask for and nobody may decline is not an affordance, it is a description." .
ag:Chosen a ag:Mode ; rdfs:label "chosen" ;
    rdfs:comment "At my discretion — the rows the deliberator ranges over." .
ag:Honoured a ag:Mode ; rdfs:label "honoured" ;
    rdfs:comment "A lever a valid presentation may demand. The deliberator passes over these — not because a duty is nobody's to decide, but because it steers a property toward an aim and a duty is a graph-shaped goal it cannot express." .

ag:mode a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range ag:Mode ;
    rdfs:label "mode" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which kind of row this is. Absent means chosen, so a branch written before the distinction keeps its meaning — but the shipped ones say it out loud." .

ag:MenuGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Graph ; rdfs:label "Menu graph" ;
    rdfs:comment """What the agent COULD do: the affordance rows, what others may demand of it, and what taking any of it would MAKE TRUE.

A ROW is derived and never authored — it is a conclusion whose premises are stored, so an authored row could outlive its premise, and that is the one hazard this modality must never reintroduce. A RULE about a means is asserted, from the file of the package that owns the lever, and is in no danger of outliving anything because it is a schema rather than a conclusion. Both are the same modality and they differ in ARRIVAL, which is what `ag:arrivedBy` is for: the two axes were separated precisely so a class would not have to encode how its facts got there.""" .

ag:IntentionGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Graph ; rdfs:label "Intention graph" ;
    ag:graphPrefix "http://example.org/orexis/graph/intentions/" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the agent is DOING: commitments adopted and not yet resolved. Small and live, because what is finished belongs to history." .

ag:PossibleGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Graph ; rdfs:label "Possible graph" ;
    rdfs:comment """What WOULD be, if the agent did. The seventh modality, and the one a-plan-is-a-path-of-graph-diffs reserved when it said possible worlds are computed and dropped: belief is *is*, the menu is *could do*, and this is *would be, if I did*.

Nothing here is believed, wanted, committed to or done, which is why it could not sit under any of the six. A reader who mistook it for belief would think an agent had observed a reading no instrument took.""" .

ag:HistoryGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Graph ; rdfs:label "History graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the agent DID: resolved commitments, discharged obligations, doses delivered, amendments woken into. A bounded ring with the series store behind it — nothing points INTO history, because whatever must outlive the window copies what it needs at commit time, which is how an expectation already carries its own baseline." .

#  --- how it arrived ---

ag:Arrival a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Arrival" ;
    rdfs:comment "How a graph's content got there. Five values and no sixth: everything in this system is said, computed one of two ways, done, or heard." .

ag:Asserted a ag:Arrival ; rdfs:label "asserted" ;
    rdfs:comment "The sovereign said so. True by ratification, not by argument." .
ag:Derived a ag:Arrival ; rdfs:label "derived" ;
    rdfs:comment "A package's rule concluded it from given facts. Policy: another society could rule otherwise with the same vocabulary." .
ag:Entailed a ag:Arrival ; rdfs:label "entailed" ;
    rdfs:comment "The vocabulary's own logic implies it — subclass, subproperty, hasValue, property chains. Not policy: any reasoner over these axioms would agree." .
ag:Recorded a ag:Arrival ; rdfs:label "recorded" ;
    rdfs:comment "This agent's own act, at runtime. Its word about itself." .
ag:Received a ag:Arrival ; rdfs:label "received" ;
    rdfs:comment "Another party's word: an instrument's reading, a peer's claim. The only arrival whose trustworthiness is a question — an assertion is the sovereign's by definition and a derivation is checkable arithmetic, but this either carries a signature or does not, which is why the graph classification and the trust story are one story." .

ag:arrivedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Graph ; rdfs:range ag:Arrival ;
    rdfs:label "arrived by" .

ag:graphPrefix a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Graph ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "graph prefix" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where graphs of this class live, for the ones that cannot be declared: a per-agent graph does not exist until its agent does, so the vocabulary states the PREFIX and the instances are found under it. What this exists for is knowing litter from property — a graph nothing declares and no prefix claims is a ghost from an amendment, and dropping it is how a volume stops holding facts in a spelling nothing speaks. Declared by whichever package mints the graphs, which is why the sweep needs no list: it asks." .

ag:PublicGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Graph ; rdfs:label "Public graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "Knowledge every agent in a world may read: the vocabulary, the topology, and what the rules and the RDFS closure made of them. This is the class `store.query` looks for — the default graph of every query is whatever is an instance of THIS, discovered rather than listed, so adding one is a vocabulary edit and touches no code. Membership is deliberately by declaration and not by exclusion: a graph nobody typed is invisible, which is the safe direction to fail — the other way round, a new private graph nobody remembered to exclude would leak into every query." .

#  The existing classes, now saying their modality too. Nothing moves: a graph that was a
#  WorldGraph is a WorldGraph AND a BeliefGraph, because "topology the sovereign ratified" and
#  "an assertion about what is" are answers to different questions.

ag:WorldGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:PublicGraph , ag:BeliefGraph ; rdfs:label "World graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "Topology and composed capabilities: who is wired to what, and who can do what. Public, sovereign-authored. Covers what the sovereign ratified AND what the rules and the closure made of it — those are three graphs and one role, because what a graph holds and who put it there are different questions. The second is answered in PROV-O; see orexis/provenance.py." .
ag:SensedGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Graph , ag:BeliefGraph ; rdfs:label "Sensed graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "What sensors read — a belief graph whose content RECEIVED, since a reading is an instrument's word rather than this agent's. Measurement only; never a judgment. NOT public: an agent's readings are its own." .
ag:OntologyGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:PublicGraph , ag:BeliefGraph ; rdfs:label "Ontology graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "The T-Box: this module and every other, plus what RDFS entails of it." .
ag:EffectGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:PublicGraph , ag:MenuGraph ; rdfs:label "Effect graph" ;
    rdfs:comment """What each MEANS makes true — one `sh:SPARQLRule` per lever, loaded from the packages at genesis exactly as the T-Box is, and replaced from those files on every boot.

PUBLIC and not belief. A rule here is a SCHEMA — what applying a lever WOULD do, stated once by the package that owns the lever — and schemas have always lived in the store: that is what the ontology graph is. It is not a belief, because nobody holds it and no agent may revise it; and it is not the menu, which is INSTANCES (this valve, this venue) and stays computed for the reason affordances are never stored — a stored row can outlive the plumbing it was concluded from, and a schema cannot.""" .

ag:DeliberationGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:PossibleGraph ; rdfs:label "Deliberation graph" ;
    rdfs:comment """What the planner considered on its last pass, per goal: the levers it simulated, the urgency each world would have reached, which one it took and why the others lost.

THIS IS THE RECORD'S ONE EXCEPTION, TAKEN DELIBERATELY AND NOT AN EXEMPTION FROM THE RULE. Possible worlds are computed and dropped, because a stored conclusion can outlive the premise it came from — except where a reader outside the process needs one, and then it is materialised into a graph of its own class, cleared at the start of every pass, carrying PROV to what generated it, never public and never in belief. All four hold here. See a-plan-is-a-path-of-graph-diffs, "Possible worlds are computed, not kept".

The reader who needs it cannot get it any other way: pyoxigraph holds an exclusive lock on the belief base, so nothing outside the process can re-run the search to see what it saw. That is the same fact `orexis-ask` exists for.

NOT PUBLIC, and the reason is not tidiness. Which levers an agent weighed and rejected is a disclosure nobody decided to make, and a rival reading it would learn what its neighbour can nearly do.

And nothing may read it BACK: cycle detection lives in the search where it can be reasoned about, not in a graph the agent consults about its own past thinking. A trace that becomes memory is a conclusion feeding a conclusion, which is the failure the computed-not-stored rule exists to prevent.""" .

#################  What the planner considered  #################
#
#  The vocabulary of a deliberation trace. It describes a PASS — one goal, the levers weighed
#  for it, and the verdict on each — and it exists so a sovereign can ask "why did you not
#  water it" and get the working rather than the answer.
#
#  Why it needs terms of its own rather than reusing the intention ledger's: an intention is
#  something an agent COMMITTED to, and every word there says so. A candidate is a world the
#  agent looked at and declined. Spelling the two alike would make "considered" and "adopted"
#  indistinguishable to a query, which is exactly the distinction a trace is for.

ag:Deliberation a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Deliberation" ;
    rdfs:comment "One pass of the planner, about one goal. Replaced whenever that goal is planned again — the graph holds the last pass and never two, because a trace that outlives its pass is a conclusion sitting beside beliefs it can contradict." .

ag:Candidate a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Candidate" ;
    rdfs:comment "One lever, simulated. Carries the world it would have reached as a number and the reason it was or was not taken — the same lever may appear twice in one pass at different depths, because what a dose is worth depends on where it is taken from." .

ag:deliberatedOn a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Deliberation ;
    rdfs:label "deliberated on" ;
    rdfs:comment "The goal this pass was about — the shape itself, so a reader can join the trace to the want and see what was being pursued rather than a label for it." .

ag:considered a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Deliberation ; rdfs:range ag:Candidate ;
    rdfs:label "considered" .

ag:chose a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Deliberation ; rdfs:range ag:Candidate ;
    rdfs:label "chose" ;
    rdfs:comment "The candidate whose world the plan commits to reaching. Absent when the pass took nothing, which is most of them and is an answer rather than a gap: `ag:verdict` then says which of the silences it was." .

#  NO DOMAIN, and deliberately: a verdict is said of a PASS (why it ended) and of a CANDIDATE
#  (why it lost), and they are the same idea at two scales. Declaring a domain would make the
#  closure type every candidate a Deliberation, which is worse than saying nothing.
ag:verdict a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "verdict" ;
    rdfs:comment "Why it ended as it did. On a pass: satisfied, improved, no candidate, exhausted, not better, refused — six answers where a planner returning a move or None could give two. On a candidate: whether it met the goal, improved on standing still, was worse, or landed in a world already seen." .

ag:wouldReach a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Candidate ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "would reach" ;
    rdfs:comment "The urgency of the world this lever would have made. The comparison the whole search turns on: a candidate is worth taking only if this is lower than where the agent already stands." .

ag:standsAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Deliberation ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "stands at" ;
    rdfs:comment "The urgency of the world the agent is actually in — what every candidate is measured against, kept so a reader need not recompute it to understand the verdicts." .

ag:wouldTake a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Candidate ; rdfs:range ag:Means ;
    rdfs:label "would take" ;
    rdfs:comment "The move this candidate is. NOT `ag:by`, whose domain is `ag:Intention` — spelling a weighed lever with the word for a carried-out one would have the closure type every candidate a commitment, which is the exact distinction a trace exists to keep." .

ag:through a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Candidate ;
    rdfs:label "through" ;
    rdfs:comment "The lever the candidate would act through — the affordance row's own `via`, which is the PROV the record asks for: a trace says which thing in the world would have done this, not merely what kind of move it was." .

ag:atDepth a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Candidate ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "at depth" ;
    rdfs:comment "How many steps into the plan this candidate sits. Kept because a lever rejected at depth 0 and the same lever rejected at depth 1 are different findings — the second says a chain was tried." .

ag:tookSeconds a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Deliberation ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "took seconds" ;
    rdfs:comment "How long the pass took. The one figure a trace could not already answer, and the one most likely to change this design: a pass validates a shape per candidate, and `series()` re-plans EVERY goal on every reporting tick, so an agent's dominant cost may turn out to be the instrumentation asking it what it wants. Recorded from inside the pass rather than timed by a caller, because a caller would be timing the trace write as well and reporting the observer's cost as the observed's." .

ag:asOf a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Deliberation ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "as of" ;
    rdfs:comment "When the pass ran. A trace is about a moment — the readings it weighed were the ones held then — and a reader who cannot tell how old it is cannot tell whether it explains what the agent is doing now or what it was doing before the last reading arrived." .

ag:blind a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Deliberation ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "blind" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether some lever was passed over for want of a stated effect, leaving the pass unable to claim it looked at everything. A verdict of `no candidate` beside this being true means the search saw part of the menu — which is why the deliberator defers to the reflex rather than reporting that nothing helps." .

ag:ProvenanceGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Graph ; rdfs:label "Provenance graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the other graphs ARE, in PROV-O. NOT public, and the reason is use versus mention: these are statements ABOUT graphs, so merging them into the default graph would make an ordinary `?device a ?class` start returning activities." .

ag:beliefsOf a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Graph ; rdfs:range ag:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "beliefs of" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which agent owns this graph — the write boundary, made explicit. The domain is ag:Graph since ag:BeliefsGraph retired (#299): the whole store is beliefs, so a class naming one graph inside it 'the beliefs graph' asserted nothing — what the graph at .../graph/beliefs/<id> actually holds is the PICK RECORD, typed ag:PickRecordGraph for what it IS, and this property still says whose it is." .

##  The instances. Data, not terms — code names the classes above and finds these by asking.
##  Three world graphs and two ontology graphs, because the role is what a graph HOLDS and how
##  a fact got there is a separate question — which each instance now answers with ag:arrivedBy
##  rather than by having its arrival encoded in its name.

<http://example.org/orexis/graph/ontology>          a ag:OntologyGraph ; ag:arrivedBy ag:Asserted .
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/ontology/entailed> a ag:OntologyGraph ; ag:arrivedBy ag:Entailed .
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/world>             a ag:WorldGraph ;    ag:arrivedBy ag:Asserted .
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/world/derived>     a ag:WorldGraph ;    ag:arrivedBy ag:Derived .
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/world/entailed>    a ag:WorldGraph ;    ag:arrivedBy ag:Entailed .
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/sensed>            a ag:SensedGraph ;   ag:arrivedBy ag:Received .
##  A BELIEF and not a reading: what the agent holds about the instruments it polls. It
##  arrives `ag:Recorded` because the agent writes it down as it goes, the way it records
##  its own classification — not asserted from a file, not derived by a rule, and not
##  received from anyone. Private: nobody else needs to know what rhythm I am running,
##  and the horizon it yields is mine to judge readings by.
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/instruments>       a ag:BeliefGraph ;  ag:arrivedBy ag:Recorded .
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/effects>            a ag:EffectGraph ;  ag:arrivedBy ag:Asserted .
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/provenance>        a ag:ProvenanceGraph .
##  One pass per goal and no more: written at the start of each, replacing what was there.
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/deliberation>      a ag:DeliberationGraph ; ag:arrivedBy ag:Derived .

##  Where an agent says what its OWN graphs are. A per-agent graph cannot be declared in a
##  T-Box — it does not exist until its agent does — so the agent classifies its own at boot,
##  and it has to land somewhere READABLE — a reader asks what a graph IS and must resolve
##  it without naming any graph instance, which is the rule that keeps a query from silently
##  reading part of the truth. (The example this comment carried, a modality-scoped
##  `?d a ag:DesireGraph`, retired with the modality classes: the desire modality is a STORE
##  now, and inside a store the graphs say only who put the fact there — #312.)
##
##  Public and belief-modality, because a classification is a fact about what a graph IS —
##  and the static graphs above have always been classified in the ontology graph, which is
##  public too. Putting the per-agent ones in `provenance` (which is deliberately outside the
##  default union, to keep mentions of graphs from answering questions about devices) was the
##  inconsistency, and it showed up the first time a scoped query could not see them.
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/classification>    a ag:PublicGraph , ag:BeliefGraph ;
    ag:arrivedBy ag:Recorded .

##  Where a world states a root desire: a TriG block in the world's own files, replaced from
##  them on every boot exactly as the world graph is, projected into the desire modality by
##  its build. Declared here so the ghost walk keeps it and so a world file needs no typing
##  line — naming the graph is enough, and a world may name instances.
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/desire/asserted>   a ag:PublicGraph ;
    ag:arrivedBy ag:Asserted .



#  `ag:Bounds`, `ag:boundedBy`, `ag:toleratedMin` and `ag:toleratedMax` were here and are GONE.
#  They said that what an agent is held to is a kind of THING with two pairs of numbers; it is a
#  kind of FORCE, and the numbers belong to shapes that already knew how to carry them. The
#  region became a shape at ag:ShouldBecome and the envelope one at sh:Warning, so what used to
#  need four terms and a class now needs none. See a-desire-is-a-shape.

#################  What an agent pursues, and with what force  #################
#
#  A desire is a SHAPE (a-desire-is-a-shape): the one language this project already had for
#  saying what a graph should look like, so constraint, desire and obligation stop being three
#  structures and become one, differing in SEVERITY and in whose graph they sit.
#
#  `sh:Violation` is the force the vocabulary already had — illegitimate, refused. This adds
#  the one it lacked: a shape whose violation is neither illegal nor merely noteworthy but a
#  GAP, something an agent is trying to close. `conforms()` has let non-violations through
#  since it was written, so an agent whose pot is dry starts, wants, and acts — which is the
#  whole point of a desire being a shape rather than a rule.

ag:ShouldBecome a sh:Severity ; rdfs:label "should become" ;
    rdfs:comment "The force of a want. A result at this severity is a GAP: legitimate, unsatisfactory, and addressed through the agent's levers rather than by refusing to run — the third point on the modality axis a-plan-is-a-path-of-graph-diffs named, finally expressible because SHACL lets severity be any IRI. It belongs on the shape that PRODUCES the result and not on the node shape above it: put it above and every unmet desire reports as a Violation, which would refuse to boot any agent whose pot is dry." .

#  WHICH WAY a want is unmet, said by the shape rather than worked out by whoever reads the
#  result. A validation result carries `sh:sourceConstraintComponent`, and after the split both
#  sides fail the SAME component (`QualifiedMaxCount`) — so the component cannot tell them apart
#  and something on the shape must. This is that something, and it is what a means will match
#  against when it declares which violations it repairs (#239): watering repairs Below, a fan
#  repairs Above, and `market:direction` stops being a hardcoded bit in a reflex.

ag:Side a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Side" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which way a want is unmet. Three values and not two, because not having looked is its own answer rather than a point on the number line." .

ag:Below a ag:Side ; rdfs:label "below" ;
    rdfs:comment "A reading sits under the floor of the region, or under the survival envelope. The side a lever that RAISES the property repairs." .
ag:Above a ag:Side ; rdfs:label "above" ;
    rdfs:comment "A reading sits over the ceiling. The side a lever that LOWERS the property repairs — and for soil moisture in this domain, no lever does: a drowning plant is a real want with no answer, which is worth being able to say." .
ag:Stale a ag:Side ; rdfs:label "stale" ;
    rdfs:comment "A reading exists and stopped being evidence about now — older than the horizon this agent publishes for that sensor. Distinct from `ag:Unmeasured`, which is having no reading at all, because the two differ in fact and an agent should be able to say which: one has never looked, the other looked and let the answer go cold. Both are repaired by the same move, and by no other — no lever moves a number you cannot see." .

ag:Unmeasured a ag:Side ; rdfs:label "unmeasured" ;
    rdfs:comment "Nothing has been read. Not a side of the range at all, which is why it is a value here rather than an absence: the repair is to LOOK, and the lever that answers it is Observe rather than anything that moves the world." .

ag:violationIs a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain sh:PropertyShape ; rdfs:range ag:Side ;
    rdfs:label "violation is" ;
    rdfs:comment "What it MEANS when this property shape is violated. On the shape, so the answer travels with the constraint that produced the result and cannot be recomputed differently by two readers. The alternative was for every consumer to compare the value against the bounds again — which is what the module, the query and the dashboard each did separately, and which is how a fern above its region came to be reported as one unmet want indistinguishable from a fern dying of thirst." .

#################  What a lever MAKES TRUE  #################
#
#  A means states its effect as a CONSTRUCT: the triples that applying it would add. The
#  vocabulary is SHACL-AF's — `sh:SPARQLRule`, `sh:condition` for the shape that must hold
#  before it may run, `sh:construct` for the query as a literal — because a rule with a
#  condition and a construct IS the action schema this needed, already RDF, already persisted,
#  already readable by a model and a sovereign. Nothing was invented here except the one thing
#  the spec has no word for.
#
#  WHY A CONSTRUCT rather than add/delete patterns, which is the textbook form: opening a valve
#  ADDS NO TRIPLE. It changes a number a later observation reports, and no template of concrete
#  triples can say that without predicting the number. A CONSTRUCT can, because this project
#  already computes that prediction for verification.

ag:retracts a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain sh:SPARQLRule ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "retracts" ;
    rdfs:comment """A CONSTRUCT whose result is the triples this effect REMOVES. Ours, because SHACL-AF has none: its rules exist to add entailments, so the standard has no way to say a thing stops being true. A simulator computes `(beliefs - retracts) + adds`, which keeps the whole operation side-effect free — nothing is deleted anywhere, two graphs are subtracted.

It is not optional, and the reason is arithmetic rather than taste. `sensed_writer` does DELETE-then-INSERT on ONE deterministic observation node per (subject, property), so a reading REPLACES its predecessor rather than joining it. An effect that predicts a new reading without retracting the node it replaces leaves that node carrying two `sosa:hasSimpleResult` values — and the survival envelope is `sh:qualifiedMaxCount 0` over readings past its edge, so a stale bad reading makes a possible world report a catastrophe in a world the plan has just fixed, and the planner rejects the plan that works. It fails the other way too: a stale GOOD reading satisfies a region goal the predicted value misses.""" .

#################  WHEN an effect lands, and how you would know  #################
#
#  A planner must know two things a rule did not say: when it may RE-DECIDE — never before an
#  effect could have landed, which states the flooding failure as a rule instead of patching it
#  with patience — and when to call an act failed, which is when "nothing happened" stops being
#  impatience and becomes a verdict.
#
#  Both figures existed and neither was a property of the effect. Actuation deadlined a dose at
#  `cmd.seconds + doseGraceS`, sensing computed `stale_after_s` from the cadence it commanded,
#  the keeper used `patienceS` and its own docstring called that a seam — "the dose and the
#  physics could derive a better one". Each was right where it sat, and a planner could read
#  none of them.
#
#  This is the argument #238 made for magnitude, one axis over. `cmd.seconds + grace` is a claim
#  about when the world should have answered; if a planner computes its own version, the agent
#  plans against one timeline and verifies against another, and the disagreement surfaces as
#  false UNMET verdicts — the same failure arriving through the clock instead of the arithmetic.

ag:landsAfter a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain sh:SPARQLRule ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "lands after" ;
    rdfs:comment """A SELECT yielding `?seconds`: how long after the act until the WORLD CHANGE is complete. A query and not a number, because the duration is a function of the act rather than a property of the lever — a valve open for a two-litre dose is open longer than for a half-litre one, and `min(litres, cap) / calibration` is exactly the arithmetic the effect rule already performs. Its parameters are the effect's own, so one text answers for any act it could be asked about.

ZERO is a real answer and the honest one for a look. Observing changes nothing about the world; what a sensing act delays is KNOWLEDGE, and that is the confirmation route's business rather than this one's. A rule that omitted this would be claiming immediacy by silence, so the shapes ask for it.

WHAT IT DOES NOT INCLUDE, said here because the name could be read as promising it: SETTLING. This is delivery — when the valve stops — and not when the world has reached a steady state a reading would report. Three terms separate an act from its evidence: delivery (here, from the device's own calibration), absorption (the pot's, and stated in NO world today), and the next reading (the cadence the agent commanded plus its grace). Only the first and third are facts anyone has written down, and the sensor's cadence has been covering for the second by coincidence — a plant in trouble reads every thirty seconds, which is longer than a small pot takes to absorb a dose. A planner that wants to know when its work would become visible is wrong by exactly the missing term, and the honest first step is that somebody must say what these pots do, not that the code should pick a number.""" .

ag:ConfirmationRoute a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Confirmation route" ;
    rdfs:comment "How an effect becomes knowable. A property of the MEANS — whether a given agent can actually take the route is a fact about its wiring (a valve with no status channel, a property no sensor watches), which the runtime already answers and this deliberately does not." .

ag:ByConstruction a ag:ConfirmationRoute ; rdfs:label "by construction" ;
    rdfs:comment """CONSTITUTIVE: saying makes it so, and there is nothing to wait for and nothing to verify. A claim is issued the moment the host issues it; a debt steps to demanded because the holder presented. The graph IS the world for these facts.

Named because conflating it with a causal effect produces code that verifies an agent really did write down what it just wrote down — and, worse, leaves a planner waiting for a confirmation that no one will ever send. Opening a valve is the contrast: it does not make a plant watered, and the world may decline to answer.""" .

ag:ByReport a ag:ConfirmationRoute ; rdfs:label "by report" ;
    rdfs:comment "A device says what it did, on a channel the world states. The dose's own report — `actuation:doseGraceS` past its landing is when nobody is going to send one." .

ag:ByObservation a ag:ConfirmationRoute ; rdfs:label "by observation" ;
    rdfs:comment "A later reading shows it. The slowest route and the only one that answers whether the act HELPED rather than whether it happened — which is why a dose has both: the valve reports that it opened, and the sensor reports whether the pot moved." .

ag:Unconfirmed a ag:ConfirmationRoute ; rdfs:label "unconfirmed" ;
    rdfs:comment "Nothing will ever say. A valve wired with no status channel and a property no sensor watches is a REAL deployment, not an oversight, and the honest response is to say so — an agent that cannot verify should stop acting on the property and restore its observation first, rather than dosing blind, which is exactly how a society floods a plant while every module behaves as written." .

ag:confirmedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain sh:SPARQLRule ; rdfs:range ag:ConfirmationRoute ;
    rdfs:label "confirmed by" ;
    rdfs:comment "By which route this effect becomes knowable. On the rule, so a planner reading the tool list learns what waiting for this act would even mean before it commits to one." .

ag:effectOf a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain sh:SPARQLRule ; rdfs:range ag:Means ;
    rdfs:label "effect of" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which means this rule is the effect of. The join a planner walks from a menu row to what taking that row would do — the row names its means, and this names the rule. On the rule rather than on the means, so a package ships one file that adds itself: nothing in the kernel lists which levers have effects, exactly as nothing lists which packages ship affordances." .

ag:holds a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "holds" ;
    rdfs:comment "What this agent is held to or holds to: an `ag:Desire` (which carries its met-shape and its measure), or a bare shape like the survival envelope, whose violation is a warning rather than a want. Replaces `ag:boundedBy`, which named only the first and named it as though bounds were a kind of thing rather than a kind of FORCE. No `rdfs:range` since the desire was reified: a held thing is a desire OR a shape, and a range naming one would quietly claim the other is not held." .

#################  THE MIND'S STATES  #################
#
#  What a mind CONTAINS — desires, obligations, intentions and the means they name — declared
#  in the kernel because they are the lingua franca: four capability packages already had to
#  name them (market's bidding and hosting, deliberation, actuation), and a term many packages
#  must name is nobody's package-private word. `ag:IntentionGraph` holding `intention:Intention`
#  was a split brain the graph axes exposed.
#
#  WHAT STAYS IN THE PACKAGES is the HOW: `desire:Deducing` and `desire:Consulting` are two ways
#  of arriving at a region, `intention:Keeping` one way of keeping a ledger, and each capability
#  keeps its own parameters (a patience, a met-fraction). That is rule 2 unchanged — a family is
#  where the how could differ — and it is why universality was never the test:
#  telemetry-is-a-mandatory-capability settled that a capability every agent holds is still a
#  capability, and the mirror holds here, that a STATE no agent is obliged to have is still the
#  kernel's word for it. `ag:Device` is kernel and most agents hold none.
#
#  See knowledge/decisions/the-mind-is-six-graphs.md.

#################  What a desire IS  #################
#
#  A NODE with room on it, since the measure landed. For a while a desire had no class at all —
#  it WAS a `sh:NodeShape` an agent `ag:holds`, carrying `ag:ShouldBecome` — and that form could
#  say when a want is met and nothing else. What it could not say is HOW BADLY it is unmet:
#  conformance is boolean while a want has distance, and a-plan-is-a-path-of-graph-diffs already
#  named the consequence — "a goal-shape must state its measure or the planner ranks repairs
#  blind". So the desire is reified: the shape's content is unchanged and hangs off the desire
#  node instead of being it, and the measure hangs beside it.

ag:Desire a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Desire" ;
    rdfs:comment "One thing an agent pursues: a node carrying the met-test (ag:metWhen, a SHACL shape, unchanged in content from when the desire WAS that shape), the urgency measure (ag:measuredBy, a SPARQL SELECT the deliberation machinery runs against whichever world is being judged), and a label a dashboard or the ask channel can print. Derived by the desire modality's build, never authored — the same discipline the shapes always had." .

ag:metWhen a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Desire ; rdfs:range sh:NodeShape ;
    rdfs:label "met when" ;
    rdfs:comment "The met-test: a SHACL node shape whose conformance says nothing is wanted here right now. Boolean by nature — it governs the outcome LABEL and a duty's discharge, never how hard to try. Validated exactly as it was when the desire WAS this shape: unfocused, over the shapes the agent holds." .

ag:measuredBy a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Desire ;
    rdfs:label "measured by" ;
    rdfs:comment """The urgency measure: a node carrying `sh:select` — SHACL-AF's word for a SPARQL SELECT held as a literal in the graph, the same mechanic a means' effect rule uses for `sh:construct` — whose one binding is ?urgency in 0..1. Our own Python runs it, the way agent/effects.py runs a rule's construct; pySHACL is never asked to.

Parameterised by substitution like an effect rule ($subject, $property, $sensed, $beliefs, $value), because it is evaluated AGAINST A WORLD: the agent's belief base for the live number, or a candidate possible world inside the planner. That is the point of declaring it — one measure, asked of whichever world is being judged, so the number the reflex once steered by and the number the planner scores with cannot be two numbers. The aim is read from $beliefs at query time, so a review that moves the pick moves the urgency with no rebuild; an unmeasured property COALESCEs to 1.0 explicitly, because this engine binds NOTHING for arithmetic over an unbound value and an unmeasured want must never read as no urgency.""" .
#
#  The INPUT is stated in schema.org's terms and the reuse is load-bearing rather than thrifty:
#  an operating range's condition says `schema:minValue` / `schema:maxValue` about a property,
#  and the deduction is MAX and MIN over those comparable numbers. What comes OUT is SHACL's
#  `sh:minInclusive` / `sh:maxInclusive` — so there IS a translation now, one, in `rules.ru`,
#  and it buys a want that the validator can check and a query can read without a second
#  vocabulary for the same claim.
#
#  `ssn:forProperty` survived both spellings. It is what a condition uses to say which property
#  it is a range of, and `sosa:ObservableProperty rdfs:subClassOf ssn:Property` makes it
#  well-typed on a shape too — which is why a reader can still join a plant's range to an
#  agent's want on one predicate.



#################  The aim — the pick inside a region  #################
#
#  PRIVATE, and the one part of desire that is. A region is deduced and public; the AIM is the
#  point inside it this agent is currently steering for, and it is the last thing left that an
#  agent decides about its own ends — which is exactly why it is a belief in the agent's own
#  graph, why review may move it, and why nothing may default it. "No preference inside my
#  region" is not a stance this vocabulary can express, deliberately: a consumer that needs a
#  point needs the agent to have picked one, and a missing belief is an error rather than a
#  default everywhere in this project.
#
#  This replaces `water:hasTarget`, which fused two things: the point an agent aims at
#  (desire's) and the input a litre-deficit is priced from (the market's). The domain keeps the
#  pricing — `water:litresPerFraction`, `water:maxValuePerL`, and the statement of which
#  property a bid is denominated in — and asks the aim of whoever provides the desire family.
#
#  NOTE ON MIGRATION: this is a structural move, not a rename, so the vocabulary successor
#  mechanism cannot carry a store across it — `water:hasTarget 0.55` has no single term it
#  became. A volume holding the old term is refused loudly at boot with the term named, which
#  is `agent/vocabulary.py`'s designed behaviour for a deletion: deciding what a dead structure
#  meant is authorship, not computation.

ag:Aim a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Aim" ;
    rdfs:comment "One property and the value this agent is holding it toward: the pick inside the region. Shaped to lie inside the region for that same property, and meaningless — refused — for a property the agent holds no region in." .

ag:aims a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ; rdfs:range ag:Aim ;
    rdfs:label "aims" ;
    rdfs:comment "Reads as the point this agent is steering one property toward. In the agent's own beliefs graph, never the world: the region answers to the sovereign's facts, the pick inside it answers to nobody — which is what makes it the agent's. Stated with ssn:forProperty and schema:value, the same two terms a region and a condition already use, so a reader that can read either can read this." .

#################  An obligation: a desire someone else sourced  #################
#
#  BOID's O, and the sovereign's reframe of #218: a claim presented against my hardware does
#  not bypass my wanting — it RAISES a want, with provenance saying whose it is. One kind of
#  motivation, one decision path, and an agent's whole conduct is desires pursued through
#  affordances. See knowledge/decisions/an-obligation-is-a-desire-someone-else-sourced.md.
#
#  The guardrail is the one every desire answers to: an agent may RECEIVE a desire and never
#  AUTHOR one. A deduced desire is derived from ranges the world states; an obligation is
#  derived from a signed claim the society issued. Both externally anchored, so the
#  self-satisfaction loophole stays shut from both directions.
#
#  WHOM I may owe is not stored at all — it is topology, derived like the broker ACL from the
#  wiring that says who may present to me (the market package's honoured.rq walks it). What is
#  stored is what I owe NOW, in a graph of my own, because a debt outlives the process that
#  incurred it: a host used to keep its issued claims in memory and forget them on restart.

ag:Obligation a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Desire ;
    rdfs:label "Obligation" ;
    rdfs:comment "A desire this agent did not source. It subclassed `ag:Bounds` until bounds became shapes and the class went; it is still DATA today — a counterparty, a claim, two timestamps — and becomes a shape itself when obligations drive acts, which is the step after this one. what it owes another, raised when the society issued that other a claim against this agent's hardware, discharged when the dose it names is confirmed. A subclass of Desire and not a sibling, because everything that reads desire should read this too — urgency, disclosure, and the deliberation that ranges over wants. What differs is only where it came from, which is what prov says." .

ag:owedTo a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Obligation ; rdfs:range ag:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "owed to" ;
    rdfs:comment "The counterparty. Always an agent this world's wiring already connects to me — an obligation to a stranger is not a debt, it is a forgery, refused before it becomes a want." .

ag:forClaim a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Obligation ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "for claim" ;
    rdfs:comment "The jti of the claim that sourced it — single-use there, single-use here, so a discharged obligation cannot be raised twice by a replayed presentation." .

ag:presented a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Obligation ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "presented" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether the holder has asked yet. An unpresented claim requires NOTHING of me — the holder is waiting for its own watch to be live — and a presented one requires acting now. It says whether a debt is PURSUABLE, and no longer how hot it is: heat is `ag:expiresAt`'s job, so an unpresented claim near its deadline is a debt this agent can see coming and still may not act on. The two were one step while a claim had no deadline to be measured against." .

ag:owedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Obligation ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "owed at" .
ag:expiresAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Obligation ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "expires at" ;
    rdfs:comment """When the venue stops holding the claim this debt came from — the claim's own `exp`, kept as the debt's. Absent where the market stated no window, and such a debt never becomes urgent, which is right: nobody is waiting on it.

This is what gives an obligation its urgency, and the reason it had to exist as DATA before obligations could drive acts. A stake's urgency comes from a survival envelope — how much room is left before the subject ends — and a duty has no envelope, so the room it has is TIME: at issue the whole window is ahead and nothing has gone wrong, near expiry there is none and lateness is real. Read with `ag:owedAt`, which is why both are kept: urgency is the fraction of the window that has run, and an agent holding only the deadline would have to guess when the window opened.

The alternatives were measured against this and both are recorded as the risk in an-obligation-is-a-desire-someone-else-sourced: a duty pinned at maximum is the honoured mode returning by another name, since it outranks a plant that is dying, and a duty with no heat at all is an agent that quietly defects while its ledger looks tidy.""" .

ag:amountL a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Obligation ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "amount owed, litres" ;
    rdfs:comment """How much the claim is for, where the good is measured. Written by the ledger when a claim is recorded and read by the market's Apply and Offer effect rules, which will not discharge an obligation whose amount they cannot see.

DECLARED LATE. This term was in use — one writer, three SHACL rules reading it — and no ontology declared it, for months. It worked because both ends spell it the same way by hand, which is precisely the accident `every-term-in-its-own-house` warns about: an IRI nothing declares is not an error, it is a pattern that matches nothing, and here it matched because nobody had typed it wrong yet. It surfaced when the concatenation that built it was renamed and the guard could finally see the sixth form.""" .

ag:dischargedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Obligation ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "discharged at" ;
    rdfs:comment "Absent while it stands. The same two-timestamps-and-a-reason shape an intention and a revision already have, because a debt paid and a debt forgotten must never look alike." .


#################  What an intention IS  #################

ag:Intention a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Intention" ;
    rdfs:comment """One commitment: reduce the gap in one property, by one means, adopted at a moment, resolved at another or still standing. `ssn:forProperty` names the property — the same term a region, a condition and an aim already use, so the gap an intention is about is joinable to the desire it serves by the vocabulary everything else speaks.



The lifecycle is two timestamps and an outcome, not a state machine: standing is `adoptedAt` without `resolvedAt`, and how it ended is `outcome` — the same shape review:Revision has, for the same reason. A dropped intention keeps its `becauseOf`, because a commitment abandoned without a reason is indistinguishable from one forgotten.""" .

ag:pursues a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ;
    rdfs:label "pursues" ;
    rdfs:comment """WHICH goal this commitment serves — the bounds an agent is held to, or the obligation a claim raised. A commitment keyed by (means, property) alone cannot tell two obligations about one property apart: a dealer owing water to fern and to tomato holds two Apply commitments that look identical, so satisfying one satisfies both and the patience absorbs the second impulse as if it were the first. That is the shape of the observation-keyed-by-subject bug (#one-agent-many-sensors), found before it went live rather than after — obligations do not yet drive acts, and this lands first so they never can while it is open.

Optional, because the first three means predate goals having names: an intention with no goal is keyed as it always was, and every old row in a ledger stays readable.""" .

ag:by a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range ag:Means ;
    rdfs:label "by" ;
    rdfs:comment "The kind of act committed to. A MEANS, not a plan: the how stays with whichever capability executes it." .

ag:Means a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Means" .

ag:Observe a ag:Means ; rdfs:label "Observe" ;
    rdfs:comment "Look: obtain a reading where the gap is unmeasured or too stale to act on. The first intention every agent adopts, because at birth there is a desired state and no observations — see the decision record. Satisfied by the reading arriving; dropped when whatever needed it stops waiting." .

ag:Acquire a ag:Means ; rdfs:label "Acquire" ;
    rdfs:comment "Obtain what would reduce a gap — here, bid in a market. Adopted when a bid is submitted, satisfied by the claim, and standing in between, which is exactly the state `bidding` used to keep as an untyped dict." .

ag:Apply a ag:Means ; rdfs:label "Apply" ;
    rdfs:comment "Spend a held claim against the world. REAL since #132, and earlier than the futures market this was reserved for — because the reason to hold a claim turned out to be OBSERVABILITY before it was temporal strategy: never spend a dose you cannot watch land. Adopted when the claim arrives, standing while the holder waits for its watch to be live (the #135 ack at the fast cadence, or the bounded wait), satisfied when the claim is presented on the market's redeem channel. The expectation on the end opens HERE rather than at the claim, because this is when the dose becomes imminent. The futures seam now merely extends the holding period this gave a mechanism to." .

ag:Actuate a ag:Means ; rdfs:label "Actuate" ;
    rdfs:comment "Move a property directly: the lever is mine and the resource is mine, so no negotiation — the ladder's second rung (#190), honest exactly where BOTH chains end at the agent: the lever chain (my actuator, plumbed to my subject) and the resource chain (drawing from a source no market offers as its lot — a pot-local pump on the shared barrel still bids, because the market is about the resource, not the lever). Adopted when the reflex picks the cheaper rung over Acquire, satisfied when the self-dose is commanded; the dose itself is still signed, still confirmed, still watched by an expectation — an unconfirmed self-dose is not a delivered one either, and the REA event stands even though it fulfils no exchange." .

ag:Offer a ag:Means ; rdfs:label "Offer" ;
    rdfs:comment "The host's move: open a round on a venue it convenes — the sell half of the dealer's acquire-then-offer, named so a PLAN can say its second step in the same vocabulary the keeper records the first in. ADOPTED since #206, by hosting, exactly once per owed round: a LOW nobody can serve defers the round and the deferral is a commitment — held only in module memory it was a promise a restart forgot and no ask could see. Adopted when the vessel is dry, satisfied when the refill lands and the round opens, recovered from the ledger at the next stock reading when the process that owed it has died. Deciding is still nobody's here: physics defers the round, the keeper only remembers it is owed — the whether-to-sell (the strategic-supplier seam) remains untouched." .

ag:adoptedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "adopted at" .
ag:resolvedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "resolved at" ;
    rdfs:comment "Absent while the intention stands. Its absence IS the standing state — no flag to keep in step." .
ag:outcome a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "outcome" ;
    rdfs:comment "How it ended: satisfied or dropped. A string rather than a class per outcome, exactly as review:outcome is." .
ag:becauseOf a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "because of" ;
    rdfs:comment "Why it was adopted, and — appended on resolution — why it ended. Prose for the operator reading a ledger, never parsed." .

#################  The expectation — the END, judged apart from the means  #################
#
#  `outcome` above says whether the MEANS succeeded: the claim arrived, the look came back.
#  It says nothing about the END — the commitment was to reduce a gap, and an agent whose water
#  never reaches the pot buys, records "satisfied", and buys again forever: transaction
#  confirmed, outcome never audited. Confidently dumb, structurally. Issue #131.
#
#  So an intention whose act should MOVE something carries an expectation: the value it left
#  from (the baseline, copied here at adoption because the sensed graph keeps only the current
#  witness — the ledger is what remembers), which way it should move (the same direction the
#  domain states on its valuation, #127), and by when. The verdict lands beside the outcome as
#  a SEPARATE fact: `endMet`. Satisfied-and-unmet is the false-knowledge signature — the graph
#  said this lever moves this property, and the world kept disagreeing.

ag:expectsValueTo a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ;
    rdfs:label "expects value to" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which way the property should move if the act did what the graph claims — a market:Direction individual, copied from the lever's own statement at expectation time so the row is judgeable without a join to a vocabulary that may since have been amended." .
ag:baselineValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "baseline value" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where the property stood when the expectation was adopted. Copied into the row, deliberately: the sensed graph upserts — one current observation per property — so the BEFORE of any before/after survives nowhere but here. The ledger is the memory of commitments, and this is the commitment's opening position." .
ag:baselineAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "baseline at" .
ag:deadlineAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "deadline at" ;
    rdfs:comment "By when the movement should have shown. The patience serves as the horizon until something derives a better one from the dose and the physics — a recorded seam of #131, not an oversight." .
ag:expectsDelta a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "expects delta" ;
    rdfs:comment "How far the act should move the property, when the actor can say — a dose of known litres through the domain's stated conversion. What the met-verdict measures its margin against (#165): a movement is the world answering only when it is commensurate with the act, and a breath of instrument grain past the baseline is not. Optional, because not every act can size its own effect; absent, the verdict falls back to exact crossing, which is the pre-noise behaviour and stays legal." .


ag:endMet a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "end met" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether the world answered as the graph promised: a reading past the baseline in the expected direction before the deadline — and, where the row states an expectsDelta, past it by at least metFraction of that delta (#165) — or the deadline passed without one (false). Absent while the watch is still on. A row satisfied-and-unmet is one false-knowledge datum; enough of them for one (means, property) pair and the affordance is SUSPECT — flagged, never auto-retracted, because deleting knowledge would be reaching down a level." .
ag:endVerifiedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "end verified at" .


#################  What this society tolerates of a commitment  #################
#
#  Three figures that were `intention:IntentionCapability`'s, carried on the FAMILY because a
#  family was the only thing there to carry them. There is no family — every agent keeps a
#  ledger, so keeping is not one way of having an ability — and they hang on the class itself,
#  which is what they were always about: what counts as the world answering, and how long
#  a commitment may absorb a repeat impulse.

ag:metFraction a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "met fraction" ;
    rdfs:comment "The fraction of an expected delta that counts as the world answering (#165). What this society accepts as evidence, not how the code is written. A quarter — generous to partially-absorbed doses, far above any instrument's grain. Chosen against the OTHER failure too: a margin so strict that honest doses read unmet would feed suspectAfter and mark truthful affordances false." .

ag:suspectAfter a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "suspect after" ;
    rdfs:comment "How many consecutive unmet expectations for one (means, property) pair make the affordance suspect. A fact about what this society tolerates before it stops trusting a claim, not about how the code is written." .

ag:patienceS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "patience seconds" ;
    rdfs:comment "How long a standing intention blocks re-adoption of the same one. Within it, a second impulse to do the same thing is ABSORBED — that is the amortisation, and with a consulted deliberator it is the cost model. Past it, a new adoption supersedes the old, which is recorded as dropped with the reason. An OPINION, so it is each agent's own belief and review may move it; the bounds beside it are constitutional." .

ag:minPatienceS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "minimum patience" .
ag:maxPatienceS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "maximum patience" .

ag:Intention ag:metFraction 0.25 ; ag:suspectAfter 3 ; ag:minPatienceS 10 ; ag:maxPatienceS 86400 .
